in this video we'll try to understand  the basic idea behind normalizing flows  a technique used in machine learning to  build complex probability distributions  by transforming simple ones in  particular they've been applied in the  context of generative modeling and as  we'll see allow us to construct models  with some nice properties so we'll first  examine the basic mathematical framework  of normalizing flows and then we'll take  a look at an example paper that uses a  normalizing flow to learn a generative  model of images suppose we have a  continuous random variable Z with some  simple distribution like a spherical  Gaussian allowing for easy sampling and  density evaluation the key idea is to  transform this simple distribution with  some function f into a more complicated  one well formulate f as the composition  of a sequence of invertible  transformations so the overall  transformation is also invertible for  any X we have exactly one Z that could  have been sampled to produce it so say  we ask what the density is of a given X  we might think that we can just say okay  P of x equals P of F inverse of X well  in general no this isn't the case it  depends on the behavior of F so to  understand how this works we can take a  look at what's called the change of  variables formula when we have an  invertible and differentiable function f  mapping from some domain z to domain x  with the distribution P of Z defined  over domain Z the change of variables  formula says that P of X is actually  equal to P of F inverse of X multiplied  by the magnitude of the determinant of  the Jacobian of F inverse that is the  matrix of all first order partial  derivatives of F inverse so what does  this mean well first let's replace each  F inverse of X with unique Z associated  with X so we're mapping X to its inverse  e evaluating the density of this Z under  P of Z then multiplying by some scalar  magnitude now we know that a valid  probability density function must always  integrate  one over its domain the magnitude of the  Jacobian determinant which basically  indicates how much a transformation  locally expands or contracts space is  necessary to ensure that the new density  function P of X satisfies this  requirement let's look at an example  when Z and X are two dimensional the  Jacobian of F at a given point in Z  gives a linear approximation to how X is  instantaneously changing with Z so  imagine we have a square region of space  in Z near this point the partial  derivatives in the Jacobian locally  define how this region will be  transformed by F for example let's say  we have a 2 for each diagonal term and  zeros elsewhere locally this  transformation is stretching both  dimensions by a factor of 2 likewise  according to the determinant the area of  the initial square region should  increase by a factor of 4 now the change  of variables formula we had previously  contains the determinant of DZ the x the  determinant of the inverse Jacobian  which is actually just the reciprocal of  the determinant of the original Jacobian  and we know that the total probability  of the blue region in Z should be equal  to the total probability of its image  under F  what the change of variables formula is  saying is that in order for this to be  true locally each point in the X region  has to have 1/4 as much density as its  inverse now we don't actually care  whether the Jacobian preserves  orientation or not for example consider  the case where we replace the  bottom-right term in the Jacobian with  negative 2  we no longer preserve orientation as the  region and X flips upside down and the  Jacobian determinant is now negative 4  not positive 4 but we only care about  its magnitude the relationship between  the densities of points in X and points  in Z will be the same so we call our  sequence of by checked of  transformations and normalizing flow  after Z is initially sampled it flows  through the sequence with its density  function being renormalized at each step  to ensure it remains valid normalizing  flows have been used particularly and  work on generative models as you've  likely seen Gemma to modeling is all  about learning to approximate some  observed data distribution say of  natural images or speech audio and these  can be used to sample new data points  with similar characteristics to the  training data conditionally complete a  sample as an image in painting evaluate  probabilities or densities of samples  and see which samples are more likely or  infer latent variables that allow us to  reduce the dimensionality of the data or  expose interval structure that enables  generating samples with specific  characteristics one way to use  normalizing flows in a latent variable  generative model is to parameterize the  likelihood as a flow in our framework Z  will serve as a latent variable with  prior P of Z and X will be the observed  variable produced by passing Z through  the by jekt of transformation F so let's  derive our maximum likelihood objective  first we take the log of both sides  distributing the log through the product  on the right hand side now we know that  the overall transformation F was  specifically constructed to be the  composition of a sequence of functions  so this law determinate term can be  replaced with the sum of the log  determinant of each intermediate  Jacobian this setup will allow us to  directly train our model via maximum  likelihood as we can compute the log  marginal likelihood exactly  now this differs from some other  prominent deep generator models such as  variational auto-encoders known as V AES  and generative adversarial networks  known as Ganz in the VA II case we can  only approximate a lower bound on the  log likelihood known as the evidence  lower bound or elbow and this is what we  optimize during training in Ganz we  actually avoid evaluation of the  likelihood altogether and instead  optimize a minimax objective involving  the discriminator and the generator in  addition the flow based framework allows  for exact posterior inference in fact  the invert ability of F guarantees that  we can find the unique Z that generates  a given X with a Vee  we have an encoder network  parameterizing an approximate posterior  as computing the actual posterior is  intractable and in a standard can there  is no encoder for inferring the lanes  and the generator is not guaranteed to  have full support over the data now we  have the sequence of potentially high  dimensional by jected functions whose  Jacobian determinant we need to be able  to compute but this can get expensive  the basic trick often used is to ensure  that the Jacobian is triangular this is  because the determinant of a triangular  matrix is just the product of the  diagonal elements so we don't have to  take into account any of the off  diagonal elements for example in the  paper  nice nonlinear independent components  estimation the authors ensure that the  Jacobian of each transformation is  triangular via what are called coupling  layers let Z be the input to a coupling  layer we first partition Z into two  subsets for instance two contiguous sub  vectors  for the first subset we simply apply the  identity copying over the elements to  the same positions in the layers output  X to get the remaining portion of X we  use two functions n ng n takes us input  the first subset of Z and then the  output of N and the second subset of Z  serve as inputs for G we can look at the  Jacobian of this transformation because  elements in the first subset are not  affected by elements in the second we  end up with all zeros in the upper right  block and with the identity in the upper  left we'll just need to make sure that  the lower right block is lower  triangular to ensure that the full  Jacobian is triangular the lower left  block will have no restrictions because  elements in the first subset are left  unchanged by a coupling later we'll also  need to alternate roles between the two  subsets as we compose multiple coupling  layers together as for inverting this  transformation well we can of course  invert the identity portion but what  about the second part this G function  well basically G is just required to be  invertible with respect to its first  argument and given the second since we  contribute its second argument in the  paper nice G is kept simple and just  returns the sum of its two arguments to  form an additive coupling later recent  papers have a bit more sophisticated  transformations but the basic idea is  the same so no matter how complex the  function n is say it's a deep neural  network the overall transformation is  still easily invertible and it's  Jacobian determinant is simple to  compute in fact these additive coupling  layers have a unit Jacobian determinant  so they neither contract nor expand  space and even a composition of several  of these preserves volume but we  actually don't want this restricted  behavior the model should be able to  perform expansions and contractions at  different points in space to allow for  more flexibility when matching the data  distribution as we start with a simple  Gaussian we won't be able to model  multimodal distributions without this  capability the model should be free to  increase the amount of latent space used  to represent high density  of the data so as we decode from  Layton's two data the first  transformation before the coupling  layers will consist of a diagonal  scaling matrix s the authors of nice  give the intuition that s is essentially  learning a manifold of the data the  smaller the scaling factor the less  important the latent dimension in the  limit as an element of s approaches 0  the corresponding latent dimension is  effectively removed the overall  objective now consists of summing the  difference between the log probability  of each element of F inverse of X and  the log of the absolute value of the  corresponding scaling factor in s now  even if the model is allowed to dampen  certain dimensions  it might seem pretty limiting to always  have a latent space of equal  dimensionality to the data which is  required for a differentiable by  injection what if we want to explicitly  enforce a small early in dimensionality  well in the paper density estimation  using real MVP a follow-up work to nice  a multi scale architectures use that  allows different sets of latent  variables to correspond to different  resolutions so Leighton's corresponding  to finer details can be optionally left  out during inference now we've  considered a case where we parameterize  a likelihood function as a flow but  they're applicable to other scenarios  where we'd like to form a richer  distribution for example we might  parameterize the approximate posterior  in a VA e with a flow to allow for  better inference so instead of say  restricting the approximate posterior to  be a Gaussian with diagonal covariance  we can use a normalizing flow to  transform this initial distribution and  to something more flexible  we can also view some auto regressive  models as normalizing flows consider a  model that sequentially samples each  pixel on an image conditioned on the  previous ones according to some fixed  ordering if the conditional densities  are gaussians we can view the sampling  noise at each step as a latent variable  this gives us an invertible model we can  obtain a uniquely in factor for an  observation given model parameters now  well that's the evaluation can be easily  paralyzed here the sequential sampling  means we can't generate new examples  efficiently combining this framework  with what are known as inverse auto  regressive flows helps address this so  normalizing flows are neat tool to have  when we'd like to track tably handle  complex probability distributions and we  only touched on a few of the ways of  using them in this video in the  description I've included links for  learning more about normalizing flows in  their origin as well as some recent  papers worth checking out  thanks for watching
 

